WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.949
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:07.430
Let’s talk about video super resolution.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:13.380
The problem statement is simple, in goes a
coarse video, the technique analyzes it, guesses

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.080
what’s missing, and out comes a detailed
video.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.060
However, of course, reliably solving this
problem is anything but simple.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.519
When learning-based algorithms were not nearly
as good as they are today, this problem was

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:31.540
mainly handled by handcrafted techniques,
but they had their limits - after all, if

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.600
we don’t see something too well, how could
we tell what’s there?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.570
And this is where new learning-based methods,
especially this one, come into play.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:46.140
This is a hard enough problem for even a still
image, yet this technique is able to do it

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.270
really well even for videos.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.740
Let’s have a look.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.770
The eye color for this character is blurry,
but we see that it likely has a green-ish,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.170
blue-ish color.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.250
And if we gave this problem to a human, this
human would know that we are talking about

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.080
the eye of another human, and we know roughly
what this should look like in reality.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:11.230
A human would also know that this must a bridge,
and finish the picture.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.570
What about computers?

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.610
The key is that if we have a learning algorithm
that looks at the coarse and fine version

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.080
of the same video, it will hopefully learn
what it takes to create a detailed video when

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.650
given a poor one, which is exactly what happened
here.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:31.290
As you see, we can give it very little information,
and it was able to add a stunning amount of

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.560
detail to it.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.630
Now of course, super resolution is a highly
studied field these days, therefore it is

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.080
a requirement for a good paper to compare
to quite a few previous works.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.040
Let’s see how it stacks up against those!

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.120
Here, we are given given a blocky image of
this garment, and this is the reference image

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.810
that was coarsened to create this input.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:58.040
The reference was carefully hidden from the
algorithms, and only we have it.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.100
Previous works could add some details, but
the results were nowhere near as good as the

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.100
reference.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.240
So what about the new method?

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.700
My goodness, it is very close to the real
deal!

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.840
Previous methods also had trouble resolving
the details of this region, where the new

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.150
method, again, very close to reality.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.780
It is truly amazing how much this technique
understands the world around us from just

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.030
this training set of low and high-resolution
videos.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:33.459
Now, if you have a closer look at the author
list, you see that Nils Thuerey is also there.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:38.990
He is a fluid and smoke person, so I thought
there had to be an angle here for smoke simulations.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.030
And, yup, there we go.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.770
To even have a fighting chance of understanding
the importance of this sequence, let’s go

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.790
back to one of Nils’s earlier works, which
is one the best papers ever written, Wavelet

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:52.790
Turbulence.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.610
That’s a paper from twelve years ago.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:00.410
Now, some of the more seasoned Fellow Scholars
among you know that I bring this paper up

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:05.940
every chance I get, but especially now that
it connects to this work we’re looking at.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:11.040
You see, Wavelet Turbulence was an algorithm
that could take a coarse smoke simulation

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.610
after it has been created, and added fine
details to it.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:20.620
In fact, so many fine details that creating
the equivalently high resolution simulation

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.120
would have been near impossible at the time.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.250
However, it did not work with images, it required
knowledge about the inner workings of the

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.250
simulator.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.270
For instance, it would need to know about
velocities and pressures at different points

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.010
in this simulation.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:41.740
Now, this new method can do something very
similar, and all it does is just look at the

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:47.640
image itself, and improves it, without even
looking into the simulation data!

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.629
Even though the flaws in the output are quite
clear, the fact that it can add fine details

00:03:52.629 --> 00:03:56.380
to a rapidly moving smoke plume is still an
incredible feat!

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:01.570
If you look at the comparison against CycleGAN,
a technique from just 3 years ago, this is

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:06.350
just a few more papers down the line, and
you see that this has improved significantly.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:11.810
And the new one is also more careful with
temporal coherence, or in other words, there

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:17.530
is no flickering arising from solving the
adjacent frames in the video differently.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:18.530
Very good.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.479
And if we look a few more papers further down
the line, we may just get a learning-based

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:28.300
algorithm that does so well at this task,
that we would be able to rewatch any old footage

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.589
in super high quality.

00:04:29.589 --> 00:05:25.810
What a time to be alive!

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:29.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

